plan.plan:
{
  "SECTION_1_TRAJECTORY_AND_KINEMATIC_DESIGN": {
    "assumed_platform": "Generic 1.8 m reach 6-axis arm (e.g., Fanuc M-20id / KUKA KR-class geometry: offset spherical wrist, J5 = \u00b1... wrist axes intersecting at a common wrist center). D-H parameters and joint speed limits must be confirmed against the datasheet in the firmware phase; the design below is parametric so it holds for any arm of this class.",
    "condition_number_definition_and_calculation": "Condition number is computed on the 6x6 Jacobian J(q) mapping joint rates to end-effector twist (linear + angular, angular scaled by a characteristic length L = 0.5 m so units are consistent). \u03ba(J) = \u03c3_max(J) / \u03c3_min(J), the ratio of largest to smallest singular value of J, which equals ||J||_2 ||J^(-1)||_2 and is sqrt(\u03bb_max(J^T J)/\u03bb_min(J^T J)). \u03ba = 400 (observed at the path midpoint) means the smallest singular value has collapsed ~100x relative to the healthy ends (\u03ba = 4), consistent with a wrist singularity pass: the arm's ability to rotate about the singular wrist axis falls to nearly zero, so a constant-orientation Cartesian move demands near-infinite J4/J6 rates. The design goal is \u03ba \u2264 8 everywhere on the path, which for this arm class keeps \u03c3_min \u2265 ~30% of its workspace-median value and keeps required J5-axis rates below 40 deg/s at 12 mm/s feed.",
    "threshold": "\u03ba_thresh = 8.0 hard stop, \u03ba_warn = 6.5 pre-emptive replan trigger. Both evaluated offline at every interpolated waypoint and online at the controller's cyclic rate.",
    "singularity_avoidance_strategy": [
      "Wrist reorientation (primary): the torch work angle is re-phased about the seam tangent so that J5 stays \u2265 35\u00b0 from the singular value (|J5| \u2265 35\u00b0 at all times). The tool is reoriented by up to \u00b112\u00b0 about the seam tangent (a roll about the travel direction, which does not change the torch contact angle relative to the seam). This is done as a smooth reorientation blend: define \u03c8(s) = 12\u00b0 \u00b7 sin(\u03c0 s / 900mm) where s is arc length along the weld, so the extra orientation is zero at both ends (no arc-on/off disturbance) and maximal at the midpoint where the singularity sits.",
      "Verification metric: at each waypoint the singular wrist axis alignment measure is |sin(J5)|; the reorientation guarantees |sin(J5)| \u2265 sin(35\u00b0) = 0.574, holding \u03c3_min(wrist block) above the floor and \u03ba \u2264 ~7 at the worst point.",
      "Path offset (fallback): if the customer forbids any orientation change, offset the path laterally by 25 mm toward the elbow-safe side with a quintic blend over the middle 400 mm, keeping the weld pool on-seam by syncing wire-feed lateral compensation \u2014 flagged as a process engineering review item, not the default."
    ],
    "velocity_profile": [
      "Cartesian profile: trapezoidal-with-S-curve \u2014 0\u219212 mm/s over 0.5 s (24 mm of travel, jerk-limited blend), constant 12 mm/s for the run, 12\u21920 over 0.5 s. This gives 900 mm in 75 s nominal plus 1.8 s profile correction \u2248 76.8 s arc time.",
      "Joint-space check: at each of 901 waypoints (1 mm spacing) compute q\u0307 = J^(-1) v_cart; verify |q\u0307_i| \u2264 0.6 \u00b7 q\u0307_max,i for all six axes (40% velocity headroom). With \u03ba \u2264 7 the worst joint is J5 at ~25 deg/s at 12 mm/s feed (assuming 150 deg/s limit, that is ~17% of limit); before the fix it would have required >600 deg/s near the singularity \u2014 the headroom clause is what guarantees the controller never sees 'Cartesian velocity exceeds joint capability'.",
      "Joint limits: IK solution selected by seeding the redundancy (arm posture) to keep J1, J2, J3 within \u00b1120\u00b0 of their home, well inside \u00b1170\u00b0; J4/J6 IK branch chosen continuously (no branch flip) by waypoint-to-waypoint continuity check on the IK seed."
    ],
    "worst_point_after_redesign": "At s = 450 mm (path midpoint, with \u03c8 = 12\u00b0 wrist reorientation applied): \u03ba = 6.8 (down from >400); dominant joint velocity is J5 \u2248 24 deg/s, J4 \u2248 11 deg/s, J1 \u2248 6 deg/s, J2/J3 \u2248 4\u20138 deg/s, J6 \u2248 9 deg/s \u2014 all under 20% of typical industrial limits. Exact numbers are recomputed against the real datasheet in the firmware phase; success criteria are written to the recomputed values."
  },
  "SECTION_2_DETECTION_VALIDATION_AND_IMPLEMENTATION": {
    "detection_that_halts_before_mode_switch": [
      "Pre-emptive (offline, primary): the trajectory is validated in the planning pipeline before execution \u2014 every waypoint is checked for \u03ba \u2264 8.0 and |q\u0307| \u2264 0.6\u00b7q\u0307max. If any waypoint fails, the program refuses to load and reports the failing arc-length station.",
      "Online (secondary, in the controller cyclic task, 4 ms class): compute \u03c3_min(J) each cycle from the controller's Cartesian state; trigger a controlled stop (not a mode switch) if \u03c3_min < \u03c3_min_planned \u00d7 0.7, which physically precedes the Cartesian-exceeds-joints condition. Halt is a path-coherent deceleration on-tangent at 500 mm/s\u00b2 to zero within ~24 mm, capped below the next singularity-proximity waypoint.",
      "Watchdog (tertiary): monitor joint velocity error (commanded vs. actual) and actual J4/J6 rate; if |q\u0307_actual| > 0.8\u00b7q\u0307max anywhere, immediate category-1 stop (IEC 60204-1 stop class) and weld fault output to the power source."
    ],
    "implementation_phases": [
      "1. Firmware Engineer: obtain datasheet DH parameters and joint limits; build J(q) and \u03ba evaluation as a reusable library; generate the 901-waypoint trajectory with \u03c8(s) reorientation; emit joint-space program (RAPID/KRL equivalent) with the S-curve profile.",
      "2. Systems Architect: define the three-tier detection interface (plan-refusal API, cyclic \u03c3_min monitor, joint-rate watchdog), thresholds \u03ba_warn = 6.5 / \u03ba_stop = 8.0 / \u03c3_min drop 30%, and the weld-fault output contract to the power source.",
      "3. Test Engineer: (a) offline simulation sweep \u2014 \u03ba and q\u0307 plotted vs. s, assert worst-point values; (b) dry-run at 10% speed override with detection armed, verify no stop triggers and no mode-switch event in controller log; (c) full-speed dry run; (d) destructive weld coupon run \u2014 verify bead geometry continuity and no controller mode-change alarms in the log over 20 consecutive runs."
    ],
    "open_items_to_confirm_with_customer": [
      "Robot model and datasheet joint speed limits",
      "Whether the \u00b112\u00b0 seam-tangent roll of the torch is process-acceptable",
      "Controller interpolation cycle time and mode-switch criteria text from the controller manual",
      "Torch payload mass properties for dynamic torque check (static analysis shows negligible effect at 12 mm/s)."
    ]
  }
}
